\title{flatcor: objective Bayes for linear regression with flat correlations}
\description{
Define what's possible, not what you expect. With one predictor, Jeffreys'
flat correlation is the only choice; with several, not everything can stay
flat. Four priors, built side by side; all but
\code{"independent_partial_r"} are R-squared ~ Beta(a, 1) with a uniform
direction:
\itemize{
\item \code{"joint_partial_r"}: every correlation matrix of the outcome and
the predictors equally likely; each partial correlation flat on (-1, 1)
whatever the number of predictors (a = k/2). One joint prior, so the
partial correlations are linked.
\item \code{"independent_partial_r"}: each predictor's partial correlation
with the outcome gets its own flat prior on (-1, 1), not linked to the
others; R-squared is whatever they imply (not a Beta).
\item \code{"flat_r"}: the multiple correlation R flat for every k (a = 1/2).
\item \code{"flat_r_squared"}: R-squared flat for every k (a = 1).
}
The default is \code{"joint_partial_r"}; a function that uses it because no
prior was given says so once per session. The validation of the
priors is at \url{https://github.com/dm-k1/flatcor-validation}. The three
R-based priors divide the total over the predictors with no preference:
P(total) x P(this combination | total) x unit conversion.
}
